 protesters were in  favor of the the coup  that's true so yesterday there uh some  protesters tried to gather of the  placebo  where many protests take place uh they  were quickly dispersed but conspicuously  they weren't dispersed by the military  they were discussed by the local police  uh with tear gas and so on so uh  certainly as of as of yesterday the the  police were not acting in unison with  the with the military but we certainly  haven't heard of anything uh like that  happening here today okay henry thank  you very much let's uh bring in nicholas  arc he joins us live now from dakar uh  nick just take us through what your  reading of this situation is  i mean this is really a situation that  president rockman  should have anticipated there's been  mounting protests against him remember  peter we talked about this on saturday  when there were protest ban protests a  peaceful ban protest uh that was bound  sorry by authorities um protesting  against the deteriorating security  situation in the country burkina faso  that's been facing a wave of attacks by  armed groups linked to isil and al-qaeda  and  pretty much the border areas of the  country are well the the authorities  have lost control in key areas  in the north and the east the area that  borders with mali and faced with that  millions of people have been fleeing  towards the capital which seems to be  the only safe place so there's been  mounting protests against the president  now he's taken some steps by uh by um  by dismissing his governments several  times in the past also replacing the  minister of defense but really uh the  court the ball is in his court and right  now what we're seeing is that the  military that had given them him an  ultimatum to try to change the situation  to give them more means that was the  main demands yesterday by the mutineers  um to give them more means to try to  fight this threat well they said if uh  the president didn't respond to their  demands that they would take over power  and that's what's happening right now  what's interesting to watch peter is  that this is happening in the country  burkina faso where the french forces are  present there was a joint patrol with  the burkina faso military earlier on  this week where a french soldier was  wounded they have their special forces  on the ground and it's unlikely that  they the french would support another  military takeover in the region there in  burkina faso peter and to put this into  context into a broader context i guess  for us nick this is not  a small country and there are large  areas of a medium to large-sized country  that were simply no-go areas for the  government and for the military as well  that's right it's a landlocked country  that shares borders with mali but also  key other uh nations in the area and and  for many years uh before this uh before  hawke mccabre was elected democratically  well we have to remember there was a  leader bless compadre who was there for  27 years and he kept that country safe  from attacks now what's interesting to  note where this mutiny started is in a  place where a key a key player general  gender a is being held he tried an  attempted coup in 2014 and he is known  as someone that has been speaking to  armed groups in the past that kept  burkina faso safe now he's being locked  in there in that camp there's also  someone that was arrested just last week  another member of the military there was  a commander in the north of the country  that was uh arrested allegedly  accused of a coup attempt against hakma  boy these are two key figures of the  military it's unclear if they have a  role in this situation but we have to  remember the context in which this is  happening three coups peter in the  region guinea now in the hands of the  military mali now in the hands of the  military and perhaps now burkina faso  there's a step back  in democracy in the region but for many  in burkina faso the military stepping in  is a 